WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.488
We have built four index structures, and each one wins on something different.

00:00:04.488 --> 00:00:06.976
So how do you pick?

00:00:06.976 --> 00:00:09.464
It comes down to three questions, in order.

00:00:09.464 --> 00:00:11.951
What is the workload?

00:00:11.951 --> 00:00:14.439
Is the data sorted?

00:00:14.439 --> 00:00:16.927
And what does it cost?

00:00:16.927 --> 00:00:19.415
Answer those and the right index falls out.

00:00:19.415 --> 00:00:22.792
Question one.

00:00:22.792 --> 00:00:26.169
Match the workload to the structure.

00:00:26.169 --> 00:00:29.546
This table is the whole decision.

00:00:29.546 --> 00:00:32.922
Hash index.

00:00:32.922 --> 00:00:36.299
Constant time on equality, no ranges.

00:00:36.299 --> 00:00:39.676
Primary keys and caches, Redis and Memcached.

00:00:39.676 --> 00:00:43.053
B-plus tree.

00:00:43.053 --> 00:00:46.430
Log N on any key, native ranges and sorted output.

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:49.807
The general-purpose default, Postgres and MySQL.

00:00:49.807 --> 00:00:53.184
LSM tree, built on SS Tables.

00:00:53.184 --> 00:00:56.560
Append to RAM, sequential flush, background compaction.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:59.937
The write-heavy choice, Cassandra, DynamoDB, RocksDB.

00:00:59.937 --> 00:01:03.314
The SS Table itself is just the file format underneath, not a standalone choice.

00:01:03.314 --> 00:01:06.691
And for full text, an inverted index, a term dictionary plus postings lists.

00:01:06.691 --> 00:01:10.318
Question two.

00:01:10.318 --> 00:01:13.945
Does it matter if the data is already sorted by your key?

00:01:13.945 --> 00:01:17.572
Enormously.

00:01:17.572 --> 00:01:21.199
Picture two dictionaries, same content, one hundred seventy thousand words over one thousand pages.

00:01:21.199 --> 00:01:24.826
One sorted, one scrambled.

00:01:24.826 --> 00:01:28.453
The sorted one needs only one thousand index entries, the first word per page.

00:01:28.453 --> 00:01:32.080
Look up coffee in one reed, jump, scan.

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:35.707
The scrambled one needs an entry per word, because words land randomly.

00:01:35.707 --> 00:01:39.334
A hundred seventy times more index for the same data.

00:01:39.334 --> 00:01:42.961
Sorting is what makes the index small.

00:01:42.961 --> 00:01:47.663
Apply that to a table and the term is clustering.

00:01:47.663 --> 00:01:52.365
A clustered index means the table is physically sorted by the key.

00:01:52.365 --> 00:01:57.067
The index is sparse, one entry per page, and a range scan becomes a sequential walk.

00:01:57.067 --> 00:02:01.769
Find the first page, walk forward.

00:02:01.769 --> 00:02:06.471
An unclustered index means the table sits in insert order.

00:02:06.471 --> 00:02:11.173
The index is dense, one entry per row, and a range scan pays one IO per matching row, jumping around the table.

00:02:11.173 --> 00:02:15.875
A table can be clustered on only one set of columns, because the physical order goes one way.

00:02:15.875 --> 00:02:20.577
Every other index on it is unclustered.

00:02:20.577 --> 00:02:24.056
Question three.

00:02:24.056 --> 00:02:27.535
Pull the structure and the clustering together and the IO cost falls out for every access pattern.

00:02:27.535 --> 00:02:31.014
Equality lookup.

00:02:31.014 --> 00:02:34.493
Hash and B-plus tree are both fast, one to four reeds, and clustering does not help, because the lookup hits one row anyway.

00:02:34.493 --> 00:02:37.972
Range scan or ORDER BY.

00:02:37.972 --> 00:02:41.452
The clustered B-plus tree wins.

00:02:41.452 --> 00:02:44.931
Find the first page, walk forward.

00:02:44.931 --> 00:02:48.410
The unclustered one pays a reed per row, and hash is unusable.

00:02:48.410 --> 00:02:51.889
Insert and update.

00:02:51.889 --> 00:02:55.368
LSM wins.

00:02:55.368 --> 00:02:58.847
Append to RAM.

00:02:58.847 --> 00:03:02.326
The B-plus tree pays for occasional rebalancing, the hash for a bucket update and a possible split.

00:03:02.326 --> 00:03:06.324
One trap to clear.

00:03:06.324 --> 00:03:10.322
That B-plus tree log N is log base fanout of N, and fanout is large.

00:03:10.322 --> 00:03:14.320
At fanout one thousand, a billion keys fit in three levels.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:18.318
At fanout sixty-five thousand, a trillion keys fit in three levels too.

00:03:18.318 --> 00:03:22.316
So log N collapses to a constant in practice, three to four reeds per lookup, whatever the table size.

00:03:22.316 --> 00:03:26.315
The real gap between hash and B-plus tree is small.

00:03:26.315 --> 00:03:30.313
One reed versus three.

00:03:30.313 --> 00:03:34.311
The useful difference is ranges.

00:03:34.311 --> 00:03:38.309
And LSM cost does not fit this chart at all.

00:03:38.309 --> 00:03:42.307
It is not a function of table size.

00:03:42.307 --> 00:03:46.305
It is the count of SS Tables you touch, bloom filters skipping the tiers that cannot hold the key.

00:03:46.305 --> 00:03:49.533
Three questions, in order.

00:03:49.533 --> 00:03:52.760
Workload picks the structure.

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:55.988
Sorted-or-not picks clustered or unclustered.

00:03:55.988 --> 00:03:59.215
Combine them, the IO cost falls out.

00:03:59.215 --> 00:04:02.443
Two traps.

00:04:02.443 --> 00:04:05.671
Log N is shorthand, not a real difference.

00:04:05.671 --> 00:04:08.898
And LSM cost is about SS Table counts, not rows.

00:04:08.898 --> 00:04:12.126
Pick by the query pattern, not the asymptote.

00:04:12.126 --> 00:04:15.353
That closes the indexing tour.

00:04:15.353 --> 00:04:18.581
Next, the query optimizer, the piece that decides which index to use for any given query.
